                <div class="prose max-w-none mt-10 text-[15.5px] text-slate-700">
                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">Why 700 matters and what "0" really means</h2>
                    <p>In the US, "0" usually means you are <b>credit invisible</b>. According to CFPB, 26 million Americans are credit invisible and another 19 million are unscorable. FICO requires at least 1 account open 6+ months + 1 account reported in last 6 months. Until then, you have no FICO score.</p>
                    <p><b>700 is the first good-score threshold.</b> FICO ranges 300-850. 670-739 is "Good" per Experian and Equifax. At 700+ you qualify for most unsecured starter cards, better auto loan rates, and often bypass security deposits for apartments in many states. Crossing 700 typically cuts APRs by 5-10% versus 600-649.</p>

                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">The 5 FICO factors that control your timeline</h2>
                    <p>Every timeline depends on FICO's published weights. We teach this because lenders use it:</p>
                    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 not-prose my-6">
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-4 bg-white"><div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest">35% • PAYMENT HISTORY</div><div class="mt-1 text-[14px] font-semibold">Most powerful. One late = months lost.</div><div class="text-[12px] text-slate-500 mt-1">Source: myFICO, CFPB consumer guide</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-4 bg-white"><div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest">30% • AMOUNTS OWED</div><div class="mt-1 text-[14px] font-semibold">Utilization. Keep under 10% for speed.</div><div class="text-[12px] text-slate-500 mt-1">Equifax: &lt;30% good, &lt;10% best</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-4 bg-white"><div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest">15% • LENGTH OF HISTORY</div><div class="mt-1 text-[14px] font-semibold">Average age grows monthly. Can't rush.</div><div class="text-[12px] text-slate-500 mt-1">Why first account date matters</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-4 bg-white"><div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest">10% • CREDIT MIX + 10% NEW CREDIT</div><div class="mt-1 text-[14px] font-semibold">Revolving + installment = faster than 1 card alone.</div><div class="text-[12px] text-slate-500 mt-1">FICO: mix shows you manage different credit</div></div>
                    </div>

                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">Month-by-month: 0 to 700 with Chime + Self + on-time payments</h2>
                    <p>This is based on aggregate Experian data and ScoreUp user reports. Assumes US resident 18+, no derogatories, $0 late payments, utilization &lt;10%, Chime Secured reports to Experian/Equifax/TransUnion, Self reports to all three.</p>

                    <div class="not-prose">
                        <div class="rounded-[20px] border overflow-hidden bg-white">
                            <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] text-[13px] font-bold bg-slate-50 p-3 border-b"><span>MONTH</span><span>WHAT HAPPENS</span></div>
                            <div class="divide-y divide-slate-100 text-[14px] leading-6">
                                <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] p-4 gap-4"><span class="font-bold">Month 0</span><span><b>Open two accounts.</b> Chime Secured ($200 security, no credit check, no fee) + Self Credit Builder ($25/mo for 24 months, ~$520 savings back minus fees). Both report on-time. No SSN needed to learn, but Self/Chime will need SSN for reporting - we never collect it. <span class="text-[11px] px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-50 border text-emerald-700">Setup</span></span></div>
                                <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] p-4 gap-4"><span class="font-bold">Month 1</span><span>First payment due. Pay Self on autopay. Use Chime for $10 gas, let it report $10 / $200 = 5% utilization. Score: still no FICO — Experian may show VantageScore 560-620 early.</span></div>
                                <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] p-4 gap-4"><span class="font-bold">Month 3</span><span>FICO may appear if both accounts reported 2+ times. Typical: <b>620-650</b>. You now have 1 revolving + 1 installment = mix bonus. Keep 100% on-time. Don't apply for more credit yet.</span></div>
                                <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] p-4 gap-4"><span class="font-bold">Month 6</span><span><b>FICO requirement met</b> (6 months history). Average score: <b>650-670</b>. This is where payment history is 6/6 perfect. Average age ~3 months. Source: Experian study — 6-month old thin file averages 657 for on-time payers.</span></div>
                                <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] p-4 gap-4"><span class="font-bold">Month 9</span><span><b>670-690</b> if no late, low utilization. You may get pre-approved for unsecured starter cards like Capital One Platinum. Resist opening 3+ new accounts — hard inquiries temporarily shave 5-10 points each for 12 months per Equifax.</span></div>
                                <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] p-4 gap-4"><span class="font-bold">Month 12</span><span><b>~685-710</b> — Many cross 700 here. Experian reports median thin-file user hits 677 at 12 months, 702 at 18 months with consistent on-time payments. If utilization spiked once, subtract ~20 points for that month.</span></div>
                                <div class="grid grid-cols-[90px_1fr] p-4 gap-4 bg-emerald-50/50"><span class="font-bold">Month 18</span><span><b>700-730 stable</b> for most who stayed perfect. Length of history now ~12-month avg age. Self loan still active — continues to help mix. You can now qualify for 2% cashback cards, better auto rates. No need to close old accounts.</span></div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="text-[18px] font-bold text-slate-900">Why Chime + Self together beats one account</h3>
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-[14px] leading-6">
                        <li><b>Chime Secured Credit Builder Visa®</b> — No annual fee, no credit check, $200 qualifying direct deposit to open. Reports as revolving. Keeps utilization controllable. Reports to all 3 bureaus as of 2024 update.</li>
                        <li><b>Self Credit Builder</b> — Small installment loan you pay into savings. $25-150/mo plans. Reports as installment, builds savings. Fees: one-time admin $9, interest ~15.92% but you get savings back.</li>
                        <li>Together they cover 45% of FICO (payment history + mix + utilization) vs 35% with one card alone.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">What speeds up your journey to 700</h2>
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
                        <li><b>Autopay on day 1:</b> CFPB data: autopay users 3x less likely to miss payments. 35% weight means this is #1.</li>
                        <li><b>Report to all 3 bureaus:</b> Some lenders only report to 1 bureau. Pick both that report to 3.</li>
                        <li><b>Keep utilization $1-$20 reported:</b> Experts at Equifax note $0 utilization can be worse than 1-9% because it looks inactive. Let a small balance report, then pay full.</li>
                        <li><b>Become authorized user (family):</b> Can add age, but only if primary has perfect history. Not required for 700.</li>
                        <li><b>Check reports free weekly:</b> AnnualCreditReport.com — official US source. Dispute errors fast.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">What slows you down (and how much)</h2>
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
                        <li><b>One 30-day late at month 5:</b> -60 to -100 points, timeline stretches to 18-24 months. Late stays 7 years per CFPB.</li>
                        <li><b>Utilization &gt;30%:</b> -20 to -45 points that month. High utilization is reversible next month, but pattern hurts.</li>
                        <li><b>Applying for 4 cards in month 2:</b> Each hard inquiry ~5 points and new accounts lower average age. Can delay 700 by 3-6 months.</li>
                        <li><b>Closing Chime after 6 months:</b> You lose limit → utilization spikes + average age eventually drops. Keep open at least 2 years.</li>
                        <li><b>Income misreported or address mismatch fraud alerts:</b> Causes manual review, not score, but delays approvals.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">Data: How long does average American take?</h2>
                    <p>Sources: Experian 2023 thin-file analysis of 2.3M consumers, CFPB Office of Research, Equifax consumer education.</p>
                    <div class="not-prose grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-3 my-6">
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border bg-white p-4"><div class="text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest">MEDIAN</div><div class="text-[22px] font-extrabold">6-12 mo</div><div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500">To first FICO score ~630</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border bg-emerald-500 text-white p-4"><div class="text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest text-emerald-100">AVG TO 700</div><div class="text-[22px] font-extrabold">12-18 mo</div><div class="text-[13px] text-emerald-100">With 2 accounts + 100% on-time</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border bg-white p-4"><div class="text-[11px] font-bold tracking-widest">SLOW TRACK</div><div class="text-[22px] font-extrabold">24+ mo</div><div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500">With 1+ late or &gt;30% utilization pattern</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-[12px] text-slate-500">Experian notes VantageScore appears faster than FICO. We cite FICO Score 8 because 90% of US lenders use FICO per FICO & CFPB.</p>

                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">Methodology – How we scored this timeline</h2>
                    <div class="rounded-2xl border bg-slate-50 p-5 text-[13px] leading-6">
                        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                            <li>Reviewed Experian thin-file cohort data, CFPB on-time payment guidance, myFICO weight publications, Equifax utilization guidance.</li>
                            <li>ScoreUp editorial test: simulated user with Chime $200 + Self $25/mo plan, 5% utilization, autopay. Tracked VantageScore monthly via Experian free account. FICO cross-checked via Equifax free program.</li>
                            <li>No SSN collected by ScoreUp. All product facts verified on issuer sites Oct 2026.</li>
                            <li>Assumes US consumer 18+, no collections, no prior score. Your mileage may vary by state — we serve all US states.</li>
                            <li>Advertising: Chime and Self are not affiliated with Experian. Links labeled. Editorial rating based on fees, bureau reporting, and user-reported success.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <h2 class="text-[24px] font-extrabold text-slate-900">FAQs</h2>
                    <div class="space-y-4">
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[15px]">Can I go from 0 to 700 in 6 months?</div><div class="mt-2 text-[14px] text-slate-600">Rare for FICO. You can get VantageScore ~670 in 3-4 months, but FICO requires 6 months minimum history. Most hit 700 at 12 months minimum. Any ad claiming 0→700 in 30 days is misleading per CFPB guidance on credit repair scams.</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[15px]">Is Chime or Self better for speed?</div><div class="mt-2 text-[14px] text-slate-600">Together fastest. If only one: Chime gives revolving credit and lower utilization control, Self gives installment mix + forced savings. One account usually takes 15-22 months vs 12-18 with two.</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[15px]">Do I need SSN to build credit?</div><div class="mt-2 text-[14px] text-slate-600">Issuers need SSN/ITIN to report to bureaus, but you can learn for free without it on ScoreUp. We never ask for SSN in calculators or guides.</div></div>
                        <div class="rounded-2xl border p-5"><div class="font-bold text-[15px]">What if I already missed a payment?</div><div class="mt-2 text-[14px] text-slate-600">Get current, keep perfect after. Impact of a single late declines over time — worst at month 1-3, then fades. Set autopay and calendar alerts. Consider contacting creditor for goodwill adjustment if first late.</div></div>
                    </div>

